# Survivors of `mutant_survey.sh ring_bench` that are decisions rather than gaps.
#
# Every entry here is a mutation the suite does not catch and deliberately will
# not. Recording that does not lower the bar. It stops the next sweep from
# re-presenting three settled rulings as three fresh findings, which is how a
# survey stops being read.
#
# All three come from one rule, stated at the top of `tests/bench_test.rs`:
# **this suite never asserts on a clock.** An assertion about wall-clock
# ordering is flaky on any machine with other processes on it, and a flaky test
# inside a benchmark harness discredits precisely the measurement the harness
# exists to produce. The suite asserts record accounting instead: offered,
# reported, received, dropped. That accounting is deterministic.
#
# The price of that rule is exactly these three mutations, and the survey of
# 2026-08-29 is what turned the price from an estimate into a measurement:
# 180 mutations, and the only ones left standing are timing.
#
# `write_nanos` can return a constant. Its own doc comment says never to assert
# on it, so nothing does, so nothing notices it stopped being written.
#
# `run_mutex_queue`'s batch trigger can flip from `==` to `!=`, which makes the
# candidate commit one record at a time instead of in batches. Every count
# survives it exactly. The same records are offered, reported, received and
# dropped, on both the roomy and the cramped fixture, because batching changes
# how long the work takes and nothing else. Only a clock separates the two.
#
# The cost is real. A candidate could silently stop batching, and the comparison
# would still read as valid while measuring something other than what it names.
# The alternative is a flaky assertion inside a benchmark harness, which is
# worse. If that trade is ever revisited, this file is where the revisit starts.
#
# NOT accepted, and deliberately absent from this list: the closing partial-batch
# count at `src/lib.rs:1056`, which the same survey found and which *was*
# defendable without a clock. It is defended now, and held by `mutant/S1` rather
# than excused here. The two files are the two answers to the same question.

ring/ring_bench/src/lib.rs:732:5: replace Outcome::write_nanos -> u128 with 0
ring/ring_bench/src/lib.rs:732:5: replace Outcome::write_nanos -> u128 with 1
ring/ring_bench/src/lib.rs:1051:31: replace == with != in run_mutex_queue
